1 REM ============================================================
2 REM  t59_usb_module.bpp
3 REM  BASIC++ USB Module Test & Tutorial
4 REM ============================================================
5 REM
6 REM  The USB module provides access to USB devices through
7 REM  three virtual device interfaces:
8 REM
9 REM  USB:    - General USB device enumeration and access
10 REM  HID:    - USB Human Interface Device (joystick, gamepad)
11 REM  USBSER: - USB-to-serial adapter (FTDI, CH340, CP2102)
12 REM
13 REM  ACTIVATING THE MODULE:
14 REM    MODULE ACTIVATE "USB"
15 REM
16 REM  USB DEVICE ENUMERATION:
17 REM    After activating, use OPEN "USB:" to enumerate
18 REM    connected USB devices. The device scans COM ports
19 REM    (Windows) or /dev/hidraw*, /dev/ttyUSB* (Linux).
20 REM
21 REM  HID DEVICES (joysticks/gamepads):
22 REM    OPEN "HID:" FOR INPUT AS #1
23 REM    GET #1, J$, 8      ' Read 8-byte HID report
24 REM    CLOSE #1
25 REM    The HID report contains button states and axis values.
26 REM
27 REM  USB-SERIAL (Arduino, retro hardware):
28 REM    OPEN "USBSER:COM3" FOR INPUT AS #1
29 REM    INPUT #1, A$        ' Read line from serial device
30 REM    PRINT #1, "HELLO"   ' Write to serial device
31 REM    CLOSE #1
32 REM
33 REM  SECURITY:
34 REM    USB access requires CAP_USB | CAP_IO capabilities.
35 REM    In SEC_RESTRICTED mode, the module cannot activate.
36 REM
37 REM  NOTE: These tests verify the module framework. Actual
38 REM  USB hardware access depends on connected devices.
39 REM
40 DIALECT "GWBS"
41 REM
100 REM === MODULE ACTIVATE "USB" ===
101 REM Registers USB:, HID:, and USBSER: virtual devices.
102 REM The module initializes device state structures and
103 REM registers with the VDev system.
110 TEST "USB module activate"
120 MODULE ACTIVATE "USB"
130 REM Module activated, three devices registered
140 ENDTEST
200 REM === VDEV shows USB devices ===
201 REM After activation, VDEV should show USB:, HID:, USBSER:
210 TEST "USB devices in VDEV"
220 VDEV
230 REM USB devices should appear in the list
240 ENDTEST
300 REM === USB: device info ===
301 REM The USB: device supports:
302 REM   dev_class: STORAGE
303 REM   capabilities: READ, CONTROL, STATUS, HOTPLUG
304 REM   Commands: ENUMERATE (scan for devices), RESET
310 TEST "USB: device class"
320 REM USB: provides device enumeration
330 REM Use VDIO_ENUMERATE to scan for connected devices
340 REM Use usb_gets() to read device names one by one
350 ENDTEST
400 REM === HID: device info ===
401 REM The HID: device supports:
402 REM   dev_class: HID (Human Interface Device)
403 REM   capabilities: READ, BINARY, CONTROL, STATUS, HOTPLUG
404 REM   Reports: 8-64 byte binary HID reports
405 REM
406 REM HID report format (typical gamepad):
407 REM   Byte 0: Report ID
408 REM   Byte 1: Buttons (bit mask)
409 REM   Byte 2: X axis (-128 to 127)
410 REM   Byte 3: Y axis (-128 to 127)
411 REM   Bytes 4-7: Additional axes or buttons
420 TEST "HID: device class"
430 REM HID provides binary read of joystick/gamepad reports
440 REM Requires physical HID device for actual data
450 ENDTEST
500 REM === USBSER: device info ===
501 REM The USBSER: device supports:
502 REM   dev_class: SERIAL
503 REM   capabilities: STREAM, BINARY, CONTROL, STATUS, HOTPLUG
504 REM   Default: 9600 baud, 8N1
505 REM
506 REM Baud rate can be changed via IOCTL:
507 REM   VDIO_SET_BAUD  - Set baud rate (300-115200)
508 REM   VDIO_SET_TIMEOUT - Set read timeout in ms
509 REM   VDIO_RESET - Flush send/receive buffers
510 TEST "USBSER: device class"
520 REM USBSER provides serial port access to USB adapters
530 REM Supports FTDI FT232, CH340, CP2102, and CDC ACM
540 ENDTEST
600 REM === MODULE DEACTIVATE and reactivate ===
610 TEST "USB module cycle"
620 MODULE DEACTIVATE "USB"
630 REM USB devices unregistered
640 MODULE ACTIVATE "USB"
650 REM USB devices re-registered
660 ENDTEST
9999 END
